WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.859
Dearest gentle viewer, season 2 of Bridgerton
is practically abuzz, or should I say awash,

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:11.210
with scandalously hidden easter eggs and secret
details you might have missed.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.660
And this author has been honing her skills
to reveal the most truly delectable morsels

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.660
to you.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:17.950
Be forewarned:
spoilers ahead!

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:23.051
There's a sneaky reference to author Julia
Quinn, who wrote the Bridgerton novels, in

00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:25.740
Anthony's list of potential brides to interview.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.510
Among the names he's written in his notebook
is Lady Julia, who's characterized as "a romantic"

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.550
and Miss Quinn, who he's noted down as "an
exceptional writer";

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:40.610
both of whom, like the other ladies, are amusingly
struck off the list by the incredibly exacting

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:41.610
viscount.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.020
Bees have been buzzing around Bridgerton since
the show began, and they're back this season,

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.809
and not just in flashbacks to Edmund's death.

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:52.360
At the races, Anthony claims to bet on a horse
named Nectar as it's

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.510
Well-bred, highly trained, and well-favoured
while Kate thinks he simply picked the same

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:02.670
horse as everyone else, but really it's another
example of the show's persistent bee imagery.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.560
Nectar is the sugary fluid plants produce
that bees collect to make honey.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.720
So, given the strong connection between the
Bridgertons and bees, it's no wonder that

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.730
Anthony was subconsciously drawn to a horse
by that name.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:18.310
And, of course, Anthony's fear of bees is
what leads to that scandalous situation with

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.350
Kate in both the show and books.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.570
As well as that, nectar is associated with
the idea of immortality as it's said to be

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.560
the drink of Greek and Roman gods, but in
the books Anthony is convinced that he will

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.030
die at a young age just like his father.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.771
This season, fans get to see one of their
best-loved scenes from the books: the Bridgertons'

00:01:37.771 --> 00:01:42.440
croquet-like game of pall-mall, which "is
less about winning than making sure the other

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.440
players lose".

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.750
It is a poor player who plays the game, and
a wise one who plays their opponent.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.270
The TV show takes two different pall-mall
matches from the novels and mashes them up

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.520
to create their own version of this fan-favourite
moment.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.299
While The Viscount Who Loved Me, the book
this season's based on, has Kate intentionally

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:03.920
knocking Anthony's ball down the hill and
into the lake, an epilogue that takes place

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.030
15 years later sees her deliberately create
a muddy puddle with her tea in order to mess

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.849
up Anthony's game, only to end up covered
in mud herself.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:18.530
As in the books, Kate is the player who gets
to wield the black mallet, nicknamed the 'mallet

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.520
of death' by the Bridgerton family.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.600
The mallet of death.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:22.819
Would you look at that, Brother?

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.030
But there's an interesting extra layer of
meaning to this in the TV show as Anthony

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.970
calls the game to an end after Kate's mallet
of death accidentally hits her ball over towards

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.769
Anthony's dead father's memorial stone.

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:39.440
Notice this painting in Lord Featherington's
study of a shipwreck in progress which is

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.900
a nice hint to the state of the Featherington
family and their finances in much of Season

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.819
2,
as well as how terribly Jack's business ventures

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:52.720
went overseas in America, and it also foreshadows
the disastrous outcome of his and Lady Featherington's

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.500
scheme to make money in London with fake rubies.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.941
After revealing Penelope as Lady Whistledown
to us at the end of last season, this season

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:06.260
we get to see exactly how she goes about running
her society gossip business, including how

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:11.590
she masquerades as Lady W's maid to bargain
with the printer of her scandal sheet.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.599
Printers in this town are ten-a-penny.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:14.519
But there's only one Lady Whistledown.

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:19.200
And the accent Penelope puts on as she pretends
to be Whistledown's maid is a nod to actress

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.020
Nicola Coughlan's Irish roots.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.650
Speaking of Penelope's secret outings, notice
that the blue hooded cape she wears to disguise

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.180
her identity during her visits to the printer
is a look that's mirrored on occasions by

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.680
Eloise when she visits the printers, for example,
to try and uncover Lady W's identity in the

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.680
final episode.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.340
Not only is that pale blue in total contrast
to Penelope's usual style, enforced by her

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.890
mother, but it also emphasises Pen's close
connection with Eloise, whose family's signature

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.090
colour is blue.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.709
On top of that, that Eloise and Pen are dressed
alike highlights the fact that in both the

00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:59.830
books and the show, some characters believe
Eloise is actually Lady Whistledown.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.269
I know it is you.

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:02.269
Lady Whistledown herself.

00:04:02.269 --> 00:04:06.290
There's a nice bit of foreshadowing during
the ball the Bridgertons throw in the seventh

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:07.290
episode.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.000
When no one turns up except the Sharmas, Anthony
decides they should still make the best of

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.000
the evening.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.000
There shall be dancing.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.450
And as the two families dance together, they
do so around the Bridgerton crest, whose Latin

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:22.620
motto means "Family Before All", a nice nod
to the fact that these two families will actually

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.699
be united by the end of the series, after
Kate and Anthony marry.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:30.970
The tulips that Anthony brings Kate after
she recovers are an interesting choice of

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.470
flower as last season his mother Violet told
him that:

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.470
Tulips.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.470
They symbolize passion.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.550
Perhaps your bride would like the same.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.940
That bouquet of tulips is also a hat-tip to
the novels where Anthony plucks a tulip for

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.550
Kate from the Aubrey Hall gardens, which his
mother has filled with them as they're one

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.750
of her favourite flowers.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:53.400
Given the showrunner's love of the '90s BBC
adaptation of Pride &amp; Prejudice, it's hardly

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.250
surprising that, just like the Duke Simon
in Season 1, this season's leading man Anthony

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.280
also gets a Mr Darcy-style scene when, after
sharing a moment with Kate, he trips over

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.410
her dog and lands in the lake.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:06.690
Refreshing indeed.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:07.690
Come now.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:08.690
It is not proper to stare.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.930
The whole scene is also a hat-tip to the Bridgerton
books where Newton accidentally knocks Edwina

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.120
into a lake and Anthony wades in to help her
out.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.390
Oh, and, Anthony gets a bonus Mr Darcy moment
when, like the Pride &amp; Prejudice star,

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:23.840
I shall conquer this.
he works out his troubles about his leading

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.650
lady during a fencing match.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.610
It is time for me to secure my final victory
for the day.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:33.400
Bridgerton's trip to the Royal Races channels
the mid-60s movie musical My Fair Lady.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:38.620
The way Kate's excitement at seeing the horses
fly by breaks through her usually very controlled

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.010
demeanour
Come now, High Flyer!

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.010
Steady!

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.010
Steady!

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:44.010
[whistles loudly]
Should we separate them?

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.710
It's all in good spirit.
is a homage to how Cockney flower seller Eliza

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:52.900
Doolittle reacts at the racecourse:
Come on Dover, move your blooming a!*$!

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:57.319
That kind of behaviour also gets Prudence
Featherington rebuked by her mother:

00:05:57.319 --> 00:05:58.699
Stop hollering like a newsboy.

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:03.630
At the start of the season, Kate's appearance
reflects both her uncompromising character

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.880
and the pressure of ensuring her younger sister
makes a good marriage match for herself and

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.729
to save the family from ruin.

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:14.550
So, generally, she has a more buttoned-up
look, her outfits are very structured and

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:19.979
made of heavier fabric such as silk taffeta,
and her hair is more controlled in contrast

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:24.690
to her slightly lighter and freer look as
she opens up and relaxes more as the season

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:25.690
progresses.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:30.259
Costume designer Sophie Canale has said she
took cues from India for some of the Sharmas'

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:33.530
outfits, from colours and prints to jewellery
design.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:38.940
Compared to the more demure Edwina, who favours
softer, paler outfits, Kate's clothes are

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.850
much bolder in colour, reflecting her personality
and tendency to speak her mind.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:49.040
Anthony's sideburns were trimmed down this
season to signal how he's changed since his

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.940
rakish days in Season 1 and is taking a more
mature view of his role as viscount with all

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.270
its responsibilities including his search
for a wife.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:01.860
For Penelope's gown at the Diamond Ball, the
show's designers have said they channelled

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.070
princess of pop Kylie Minogue's showgirl look
from her Greatest Hits/Homecoming tour.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:12.270
Like Kylie's outfit, Penelope's shimmering
dress has a wealth of stars across the torso

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.419
and there are even more in her hair.

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:19.110
While Pen's costume is in her signature yellow,
rather than blue like Kylie's, curiously in

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.669
the moonlight, some of the stars on Pen's
dress do actually look blue!

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:27.860
Just as each of the show's families has their
own distinctive colours for their costumes,

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.940
the same is true of their houses.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.770
Check out the Bridgertons' home and you'll
see that the palette of blues, silvers, and

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.430
pastels in their outfits is also reflected
throughout their house, from the sofas and

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:44.650
chairs to the rugs, curtains, ornaments, tea
sets, much of the clothing in various portraits,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:48.190
the fireplace, and even the family carriage!

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.199
When it comes to the Featheringtons, their
ostentatious fashion choices are all about

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:54.599
standing out
Look at me, everyone!

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:59.710
and so is their home, with its emphasis on
lots and lots of gold, and that includes their

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:00.889
social gatherings.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:03.099
Gold flowers, gold drinks, gold everywhere.

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:08.669
And their home also features rich greens and
plenty of citrus tones, especially yellows.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:13.840
Then there's Lady Danbury's house which complements
both her own style and her guests, the Sharmas,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.780
with shades of pink, peach, and gold.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:20.449
Season 2 finds Eloise settled in her sister
Daphne's old bedroom.

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:25.460
So, there's plenty of books, as she's much
more interested in literature than the marriage

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:26.780
mart;
Might I go and read now?

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:32.270
And you'll also spot a portrait of Mary Wollstonecraft,
whose early feminist works Eloise has been

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.300
reading, preferring them to Whistledown's
gossip:

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:39.370
My own sex, I hope, will excuse me if I treat
them like rational creatures, instead of flattering

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.209
their fascinating graces.

00:08:41.209 --> 00:08:42.209
Wollstonecraft.

00:08:42.209 --> 00:08:47.361
Despite her protests, Eloise begins the season
trussed up to make her society debut, but

00:08:47.361 --> 00:08:57.019
she still manages to maintain a sense of her
more rebellious nature in her everyday attire.

00:08:57.019 --> 00:09:02.360
We often see her in striped or checked patterns
as well as waistcoats and spencer jackets,

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.910
giving her costumes a more "masculine" twist,
according to the show's designer.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:11.380
There's a sneaky little meta-joke hidden among
Lady Whistledown's Society Papers when the

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:16.880
queen of gossip takes a moment off from reporting
the ton's tittle-tattle to write a book review.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.330
When Lady W describes the difference between
the author's previous books compared to his

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:25.550
latest, it's almost as if she's describing
the difference between the two seasons of

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:26.910
Bridgerton itself.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:31.990
So, Season 1 was more elusive and subtle,
leaving viewers "constantly guessing" Whistledown's

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:32.990
identity.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:37.240
However, with that already unveiled to the
audience, in Season 2 there's considerably

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:41.490
"less guessing and more waiting for the other
characters to become aware of the events"

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:42.730
that we already know!

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:43.730
Well that would explain the ink all over her
fingers.

